":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1507379180,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMzNDkzMTYwNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1883#issuecomment-334931605"},"message":"Now: There is already some splitting elsewhere as well, remember doing it for get. \nSplitting in the decorator sounds like a great idea, which should probably cover many cases, but\n- ideally shouldn't be done for the ones with batched execution\n- might not cover the cases where list of parts is first expanded to eg figure out target size of download, and that expanded list is the one passed to call","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1507379453,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMzNDkzMTg1Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1883#issuecomment-334931852"},"message":"And right it is the #1865 which got more into the performance side discussion\nIdeally, commands should just use batched version I guess, which would avoid any limits issue. But iirc some annex commands still don't have it","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1541432171,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzNTkxOTIzMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1883#issuecomment-435919230"},"message":"My two cents: I don't think a generalized solution in some decorator will cur the mustard.\n\nDepending on the specific use case the solution will have to be different. For `add` and `get` type scenarios  it will be fine to inspect `os.sysconf('SC_ARG_MAX')` to anticipate a long cmdline and split it into two or more calls. But cases like a massive partial commit (a commit with paths specified) will be harder to deal with. Here one would want to make two commits initially and then squash them, or make one commit and ammend that one with further commit calls. In either case the solution will be specific to commit. Furthermore, if a decorator would decided how often a function is called, it is better made sure that the inner function does nothing but running the cmdline, as any further setup would be duplicated between those calls.\n\nThe me it sounds like `git_custom_command` or some friend of it, need to get a parameter to split a call into a sequence of calls, if the caller states that it is safe to do so.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1541432171,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE0ODE1NDQ0Mw=="},"target":"b72ff9e04e1f5bd22d55d266b1d8e7f5d6db333a75d0a848d4bdaa5d611b5389","message":"My two cents: I don't think a generalized solution in some decorator will cut the mustard.\n\nDepending on the specific use case the solution will have to be different. For `add` and `get` type scenarios  it will be fine to inspect `os.sysconf('SC_ARG_MAX')` to anticipate a long cmdline and split it into two or more calls. But cases like a massive partial commit (a commit with paths specified) will be harder to deal with. Here one would want to make two commits initially and then squash them, or make one commit and ammend that one with further commit calls. In either case the solution will be specific to commit. Furthermore, if a decorator would decided how often a function is called, it is better made sure that the inner function does nothing but running the cmdline, as any further setup would be duplicated between those calls.\n\nThe me it sounds like `git_custom_command` or some friend of it, need to get a parameter to split a call into a sequence of calls, if the caller states that it is safe to do so.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1541432225,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE0ODE1NDgyMw=="},"target":"b72ff9e04e1f5bd22d55d266b1d8e7f5d6db333a75d0a848d4bdaa5d611b5389","message":"My two cents: I don't think a generalized solution in some decorator will cut the mustard.\n\nDepending on the specific use case the solution will have to be different. For `add` and `get` type scenarios  it will be fine to inspect `os.sysconf('SC_ARG_MAX')` to anticipate a long cmdline and split it into two or more calls. But cases like a massive partial commit (a commit with paths specified) will be harder to deal with. Here one would want to make two commits initially and then squash them, or make one commit and ammend that one with further commit calls. In either case the solution will be specific to commit. Furthermore, if a decorator would decided how often a function is called, it is better made sure that the inner function does nothing but running the cmdline, as any further setup would be duplicated between those calls.\n\nTo me it sounds like `git_custom_command` or some friend of it, need to get a parameter to split a call into a sequence of calls, if the caller states that it is safe to do so.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1541433556,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzNTkyODI4MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1883#issuecomment-435928280"},"message":"FWIW, we already deal/split in `_run_annex_command_json` depending on our hardcoded `CMD_MAX_ARG` in datalad/utils.py (I guess I didn't know about `os.sysconf('SC_ARG_MAX')` which we should use there instead)\nindeed for core git invocations solution is doomed to become smarter.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1541434243,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzNTkzMTExNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1883#issuecomment-435931117"},"message":"Will look into this, but I had specifically `_run_annex_command_json` crash on my due to long cmdlines, so whatever it does is not yet the real thing.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf